<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Revolutionary Government (Grenada)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1979">1979</a>–<a href="/years/1983">1983</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's Revolutionary Government was the socialist government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a> formed after the New Jewel Movement under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-bishop">Maurice Bishop</a> overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-gairy">Eric Gairy</a> on 13 March 1979. It built close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and began an international airport at Point Salines. In October 1983 a split within its leadership led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>'s execution, followed by the United States-led invasion of Grenada.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peoples-revolutionary-government-grenada.jpg" alt="People's Revolutionary Government (Grenada)" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See file history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Grenada.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> island state of 350 km2 in the Lesser Antilles, became an associated state in 1967 and gained independence from the United Kingdom in 1974 under Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-gairy">Eric Gairy</a> of the Grenada United Labor Party, whose government drifted towards authoritarian rule. A new generation of middle-class intellectuals, many trained at British and American universities, returned as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-power-movement">Black Power movement</a> spread through the Caribbean. The New JEWEL Movement built a following with its weekly paper, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-coard">Bernard Coard</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>'s childhood friend, returned in September 1976 the party moved towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>. Early in 1979 Gairy planned to arrest its leaders, who went into hiding before taking power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1715">Fifteen</a> days after taking power the government declared the constitution suspended and Parliament dissolved, taking executive and legislative powers for itself and postponing the elections it had announced. People's Law No. 8 allowed the internment of anyone suspected of threatening public security, and by April 1979 there were 80 political prisoners. After a plot in November 1979 and a bomb in June 1980 that killed three young girls at a meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> addressed, the government turned against its enemies. It nationalised much of trade, infrastructure and non-agricultural business while farming stayed private. Per capita growth continued in 1981, according to the World Bank, and unemployment fell from 49% in 1979 to 14% in 1983.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The government quickly formed secret links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, which supplied weapons, and through Cuba made agreements with the USSR, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>. Relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> worsened after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> backed the Soviet intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> early in 1980, and the airport built with Cuban help alarmed the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a>. Within the party a power struggle grew between the popular Bishop and Coard, who favoured democratic centralism.</p><p>On returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> on 8 October 1983, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> was accused of spreading rumours of a plot against him and placed under house arrest with Jacqueline Creft, his companion and Minister of Education. After his killing on 19 October, Hudson Austin headed a Revolutionary Military Council until the invasion of 25 October. The airport, completed after the fall of the government, was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-bishop-international-airport">Maurice Bishop International Airport</a> in 2009, and the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a> 17 convicted for the putsch and Bishop's death were released in 2009.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's Revolutionary Government” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 13 March 1979 the New Jewel Movement led by Maurice Bishop overthrew Gairy and formed the <b>People's Revolutionary Government</b>, the first revolution in the English-speaking Caribbean.</blockquote><a href="/state/grenada">Grenada</a></li></ul></section>
